WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.580
Where in the cell does Hedgehog signalling happen?

2
00:00:07.420 --> 00:00:09.360
The first clue came from a screen.

3
00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:15.400
In 2003, Kathryn Anderson's laboratory studied mouse mutants from a chemical

4
00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:16.540
mutagenesis screen.

5
00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:20.100
Two had defects in the neural tube and limbs.

6
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:24.160
Those defects pointed to broken Sonic hedgehog signalling.

7
00:00:25.133 --> 00:00:30.113
The damaged genes encoded intraflagellar transport proteins.

8
00:00:30.573 --> 00:00:36.353
That is the shuttle of motor proteins that builds the primary cilium, carrying parts

9
00:00:36.353 --> 00:00:37.913
up and down its shaft.

10
00:00:39.153 --> 00:00:43.493
Crosses placed their action after Patched and before the target genes.

11
00:00:44.273 --> 00:00:47.813
But a position in a sequence is not a place in the cell.

12
00:00:48.683 --> 00:00:51.123
The place came in 2005.

13
00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:57.023
Kevin Corbit, in Jeremy Reiter's laboratory, followed a tagged Smoothened.

14
00:00:57.483 --> 00:01:00.423
The tag let them watch where that protein went.

15
00:01:01.343 --> 00:01:06.103
When the pathway was switched on, the protein collected in the primary cilium.

16
00:01:06.917 --> 00:01:12.097
Then the group changed the short stretch of Smoothened that sends it there.

17
00:01:12.637 --> 00:01:15.277
That protein stayed in the body of the cell.

18
00:01:15.937 --> 00:01:18.237
It could no longer pass the signal on.

19
00:01:18.997 --> 00:01:21.697
The protein lost its place and its job.

20
00:01:21.697 --> 00:01:26.577
So in vertebrates, the signal is read in the primary cilium.
